{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Arthur blinked, the harsh sunlight feeling…different. Not unpleasant, just…rendered. He sat up in bed, the familiar floral pattern of his wallpaper unnervingly crisp, the dust motes dancing in the light with a distinct, almost programmed quality. He reached for his glasses, and as he did, a notification box briefly flickered in his vision: \"Inventory: Glasses (Standard)\". A chill snaked down his spine. The world felt too perfect, too clean, too…scripted. He walked to the window, gazing at the perfectly manicured lawns and identical houses of his suburban neighborhood. A robin perched on a branch, its movements precise and predictable. He tried to recall the events of yesterday, but it was like watching a corrupted save file – fragmented, incomplete, and strangely lacking in genuine emotion. This wasn't real. It couldn't be.\n\n***\n\nPanic clawed at his throat. He ran outside, desperately searching for a glitch, a seam in the fabric of reality. He bumped into Mrs. Henderson, his neighbor, who smiled with unnerving cheerfulness. \"Good morning, Arthur! Lovely day for a stroll, isn't it?\" Her voice was a little too high-pitched, her smile a little too wide. As she walked past, a small text box appeared above her head: \"NPC: Mrs. Henderson (Friendly)\". He stared, dumbfounded. He was trapped in a video game. He had to find a way out, to break the code, to escape this meticulously crafted prison. But how does one escape a world built on lines of code? The weight of the realization crashed down on him, heavier than any game over screen.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Arthur", "logprob": -0.16696929931640625}, {"token": "▁blinked", "logprob": -0.4622840881347656}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0001805857609724626}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.07861142605543137}, {"token": "▁harsh", "logprob": -1.166972279548645}, {"token": "▁sunlight", "logprob": -0.0